In a small #bowl{}, combine @water{2%cups}(warm, about 105°F), @active dry yeast{1%tablespoon}, and @sugar{2.5%teaspoons}. Let sit ~{5%minutes}, or until it begins to foam.

In a #stand mixer{} fitted with a paddle attachment, stir together @flour{2%cups} and @salt{2.5%teaspoons}. 

Stir in the yeast mixture on medium-low speed. 

Knead in @flour{3%cup} in 1/2 cup increments until the dough is smooth but not sticky. Add more or less flour as needed, use your best judgement.

Rub @olive oil{1%teaspoon} around the dough ball, cover the bowl with a towel and let rest ~{15-60%minutes}. More time is obviously preferred.

Turn the dough onto a well-floured surface and divide it in half. Set one half aside. 

Roll the other half into a rectangle (about 15 inches). 

Starting from the long side, roll the dough into a cylinder.

Turn both ends in and pinch the seams closed. 

Round the edges and place onto a #baking sheet{}. 

Repeat with the second dough ball. 

Make three diagonal cuts across the top of each loaf. 

Cover loaves lightly with a towel. Let rise ~{30-60%minutes} (the longer the better, if you have the time).

Preheat the oven to 400°F. 

Optionally, combine @?egg white{1}(whisked) and @water{1%tsp} and then brush the top of the dough for a crispier crust.

Line a #baking sheet{} with a silicone mat or parchment paper. Bake ~{17-23%minutes}, or until the tops are golden brown. When you knock on it, it should sound hollow. If it's browning too fast, lightly cover with foil and lower the temperature to 375°F.

> You can add a few ice cubes to the oven to help trap the steam and add a few minutes onto the cook time for an egg-less crispier crust.

Brush the top with @?butter{}(melted), if desired for a softer more flavorful crust.